{
  "description": "Qwen3 training peaks on an RTX 5070 Laptop (8 GB), with the length of the longest training row of each run. They show what `max_length x batch` over-prices when the data is short, and how the estimate priced at the data's own length compares with the peaks. tests/test_vram_estimate_realised_length.py prices each run through profiler.profile_from_config. `rows` and `downstream_added_rows` were measured by a downstream application with its own runner; `bench_rows` were measured by the maintainers with `soup bench train` (torch's allocated and reserved peaks) beside a 200 ms whole-card nvidia-smi sampler. Every 4-bit row ran before #2609 was fixed (peft's fp32 embeddings and head kept on a bf16 card), so the tests price those rows with kbit_fp32. post_2683_rows are the same recipes re-run by the maintainers with soup bench train on origin/main a6f5e7246, after #2683 kept the embeddings and head in bf16 on a bf16 card (a 4-bit run no longer matches the 4-bit rows above); downstream_allocated_rows are two runs of the downstream runner, on its pre-#2683 engine, that logged max_memory_allocated. capped_rows are the same recipes re-run with torch's allocator capped (torch.cuda.set_per_process_memory_fraction) at origin/main a6f5e7246: each row is one cap and whether the run finished its steps (trains) or hit an out-of-memory error (oom); device_mib is the whole-card maximum, 200 ms sampler. They show what the run needs when the allocator has to give back what it only caches, as opposed to uncapped_row's peaks (which keep that cache). kbit_probe is torch's memory around the 4-bit preparation.",
  "hardware": "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB, Windows 11 Pro",
  "stack": {
    "torch": "2.14.0+cu130",
    "downstream_soup_cli": "0.73.3 (a downstream build)",
    "bench_soup_cli": "origin/main 945a385dc"
  },
  "peak_kinds": {
    "torch_reserved_mib": "torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved() at the end of the downstream application's training run, MiB. It includes the caching allocator's slack and excludes the CUDA context. Reserved peaks of different runners are not comparable to better than about 10%: one recipe and data file gave 3388 MiB in the downstream runner and 3732 MiB in `soup bench train` (M3).",
    "max_memory_allocated_bytes": "torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() from `soup bench train` (bench_rows only): the quantity the estimate's coefficients were calibrated on.",
    "device_mib": "whole-card nvidia-smi memory.used, maximum over the run's window, MiB. Sampling: bench_rows every 200 ms; downstream rows about every 5 s for qa7-helpdesk-train-r16, every 1 s (gaps up to 2 s) for B1-B3, not described for the two device-only rows, so the downstream readings are lower bounds. device_clean is true only when nothing else was on the card (baseline 0 MiB before the run)."
  },
